rota lap 4195, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Threshold plus its three seats read whole: the compass, the Lindy effect, the grain and the crossing. The row's own strand named the lap before I had a candidate -- a guard that cannot red guards nothing, seated at the fusion of 20260802 -- and its sense decided how to look. Listening rather than scanning means asking what a page is saying underneath its words, and tools/fixtures/l/living_docs_lint_roster.sh says REDS %170 in its header and contradicts it eleven lines down.
think Opened with the scoped cold roster: 159 guards, 155 green, 1 red -- standing_equipment reading five cadence reds a peer recorded at 20260907.101056, the same condition as my last lap and none of them mine.
think Followed my own last lap rather than starting fresh. That lap closed a scan that printed a swept tree's bytes when fed nothing; the natural next question is whether its family holds another reading whose silence proves nothing.
think Read living_docs_lint_scan.sh first. Its whole-roster-empty case is already handled at line 37 -- ADVISE, exit 0 -- so the caller is honest. Then read the roster generator it calls, and the fault was in the generator's own header.
think The roster holds eleven hand-named paths in three blocks. Four hammocks and four pins are echoed unconditionally; three module doors -- rye, rishi, aurora, whose sources sit one level down where the discovery rule cannot reach -- were written for f in ...; do [ -f "$f" ] && echo "$f"; done.
think phantom in living_docs_roster_scan.sh reads the roster's OUTPUT, so a line that declines to print when its page moves can never become a phantom. unrostered is built from the discovery rule, which by definition cannot see these three. Both gates blind for the same three paths.
think Proved it rather than argued it: a pen with one real door and one absent path held behind the test printed front_doors=1 rostered=1 unrostered=0 phantom=0 verdict=ok.
obs REDS %187 wrote this line, and its own repair sentence quotes %170 -- a page dropping off a meter is a page whose pass nobody witnessed -- as the reason the three stay listed by hand. The repair understood the law and then spelled the drop-off it was preventing.
obs The pair of pen cases is the whole proof. Case 17 plants the departing spelling and shows it going quiet; case 18 plants the same missing page named plainly and shows it bitten. Without 17 this repair reads as a rewording.
obs Live reading does not move: roster still 60 paths, phantom=0, unrostered=0. What changes is that a move from here reds on the lap it lands rather than silently shrinking the corpus.
obs The REDS pin took the row at 40,848 of 40,960 -- 112 bytes free. I wrote the row shorter twice rather than fold a peer's shelf; the next row on this pin will want a fold first.
obs The send met the spine's eighth firing. I booked off the fetched spine at 579, xy moved twice while the lap ran, and the rebase left the number bound upstream -- renumbered to 582 and then, when a second rebase met a peer holding that very number, to 583 -- the stamp unmoved through both, which is the law working rather than failing.
obs The rebase also brought upstream's REDS at 40,378 bytes, so my 2,335-byte row put the pin 1,754 over. Folded row %576 -- my own last lap, BOOKED -- to construction/archive/REDS-a-reading-of-nothing-rows-576.md through reds_fold.sh, which wrote the recital line itself. Pin 42,714 -> 38,636.
